  "tags": [],
  "title": "Exploring Oxidation-Reduction Reactions",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": " "
              },
              {
                "strong": true,
                "text": " To view a short video on how to use the virtual lab, click on the link below."
              }
            ],
            "id": "jdj7rcdiho0kedm",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": " "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Using ChemCollective Virtual Lab"
                  }
                ],
                "href": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nwjhk9rkubY",
                "type": "a"
              },
              {
                "text": " "
              }
            ],
            "id": "d7dn5smgekuyee9",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Oxidation and reduction reactions have been known for millennia but were not understood until the 17th century. The terms come from mettallurgy. Most metals do not naturally exist in their metallic forms (except gold and silver), but were extracted from rock and minerals. As such the ores were \"reduced\" to a small amount of metal from a large amount of ore. It was noted that the metals would react with oxygen and form a new substance and hence were oxidized."
              }
            ],
            "id": "c5fuzpx81ge8r0t",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "We now understand that redox (oxidatioin-reduction) reactions involve the transfer of electrons. Consider, for instance, the reaction between copper ions (Cu"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": "("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "aq"
              },
              {
                "text": ")) and Zinc metal (Zn("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "text": ")). The subscript ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "aq"
              },
              {
                "text": ") on the Cu"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": " stands for \"aqueous\" and means that the ion is dissolved in water. The subscript ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "text": ") on Zn means that the Zinc metal is solid. These react according to the chemical reaction: "
              }
            ],
            "id": "2jgchtohhvoxqlx",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "42jakufy6lyr4u3",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "0wg5q5vc2vbrhqi",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(aq)\\;+\\:\\mathrm{Zn}(s)\\;\\;\\rightarrow\\;\\;\\mathrm{Cu}(s)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}(aq)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Electrons were exchanged in this reaction, making it a redox reaction. To make the electrons exchange more apparent, we can break the reaction into \"half reactions\": "
              }
            ],
            "id": "29nia1n8o8w1bze",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "3dz9fluldyr3vlc",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "i5t2bz2nhrjwfhs",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{Zn}(s)\\;\\;\\rightarrow\\;\\;\\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}(aq)\\;+\\:2\\;\\mathrm e^-\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;(\\mathrm{zinc}\\;\\mathrm{metal}\\;\\mathrm{gives}\\;\\mathrm{up}\\;\\mathrm{electrons})\\\\\\\\\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(aq)\\;+\\;2\\;\\mathrm e^-\\;\\;\\rightarrow\\;\\;\\mathrm{Cu}(s)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;(\\mathrm{copper}\\;\\mathrm{ion}\\;\\mathrm{gains}\\;\\mathrm{electrons})",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Substances that gain electrons are said to be reduced and substances that give up electrons are oxidized. So in the above reaction, Zn("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "text": ") is oxidized and Cu"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": " is acting as the oxidizing agent. Conversely, Zn is causing Cu"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": "("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "aq"
              },
              {
                "text": ") to be reduced, so Zn is a reducing agent."
              }
            ],
            "id": "j0ta9i82if5t27m",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Reactions such as that between Zn("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "text": ") and Cu"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": "("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "aq"
              },
              {
                "text": ") only occur in one direction. In other words, the followin reaction does not occur:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "f0snmlsmjjrw0v5",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "8hatpk4h8d1dm86",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "qxw947uxcv8rssg",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{Cu}(s)\\;+\\:\\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}(aq)\\;\\;\\rightarrow\\;\\;\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(aq)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\lbrack\\mathrm{DOES}\\;\\mathrm{NOT}\\;\\mathrm{OCCUR}\\rbrack",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In other words, Zn("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "s"
              },
              {
                "text": ") is able to reduce Cu"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": "("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "aq"
              },
              {
                "text": "), but Cu(s) is not able to reduce Zn"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": "("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "aq"
              },
              {
                "text": "). We can summarize this by saying that Zn is a stronger reducing agent than Cu."
              }
            ],
            "id": "g5xav992aid13kw",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "For this lab you will be mixing each of the solid metals with the metal ion solutions to determine if a reaction occurs. Use the report sheet to record your observations and answer additional questions about the reactions you observe."
              }
            ],
            "id": "elotpoz2szdbw47",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
